    {
      "kind": "source",
      "text": "An interaction system has agents and interaction rules. An agent is a small node with sockets called ports. One socket is special—the principal port—and that is what can start a rewrite. The rest are auxiliary ports; they carry the surrounding wiring through the rewrite.",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000090"
      ],
      "defines": [],
      "requires": [],
      "mentions": [],
      "fills": [],
      "need": "",
      "gapType": null,
      "origin": "source",
      "chunkId": "c0001-flat-06",
      "annotations": [
        "repair_source_fallback"
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "source",
      "text": "When two agents meet principal-to-principal, they form an active pair. A rule rewrites that pattern by reconnecting only the wires attached right there. The pair and those nearby wires are the rule’s entire input.",
      "sourceUnitIds": [
        "u000091"
      ],
